{
  "sentiment": "positive",
  "review": "I found 'Still Crazy' to be marvelousingly entertaining, and not only to those of us who lived through that raucous era of late '60s, early '70s rock. My 15 year old daughter watches it with me every time I drag out the DVD (don't worry, it's only been three times) and she loves it too.<br /><br />It is a truly loving, poignant and hilarious nod to the era, and every actor hits his/her notes with perfection. It was my first introduction to Bill Nighy and I am glad his somewhat similar turn in 'Love Actually' brought him more attention. Bruce Robinson was incredible as Brian, bringing real life to what could have been a caricature of the drug-damaged rocker stereotype. It was interesting to see that Robinson has made quite a name for himself as a writer.<br /><br />I live in Sherman Oaks, California, and after the first time I saw the movie I bumped into Billy Connolly at the local mall (he lived here at the time) and we had a brief conversation. He struck me as kind and approachable, even though he was famous for his roles in films like 'Casablanca' and 'The Fugitive.' We laughed and exchanged pleasantries, and I couldn't help but feel that we were meeting two people who shared a common love of cinema.<br /><br />Speaking of movies, 'Still Crazy' is a film that captures the spirit of its time. It's a reminder of how powerful cinema can be in reflecting the cultural moment it was made in. The dialogue is sharp and witty, and the performances are stellar, especially from Bill Nighy and Bruce Robinson. It's a must-watch for anyone who loves films from the '70s.<br /><br />Additionally, 'Still Crazy' has a unique energy about it that sets it apart from other films of its genre. The cinematography is top-notch, and the score really enhances the overall experience. I can't recommend this film enough to fans of classic cinema who appreciate well-crafted stories with strong performances."
