{
  "sentiment": "negative",
  "review": "Being a gay man who lived through the time period examined in this tedious documentary, I was eager to see how the subject matter was handled. Unfortunately, the film makers wasted what could have been an energetic and insightful opportunity to shed some light on our collective gay history. This film only concerns itself with New York City, ignoring the rest of the country. While I spent a fair amount of time in NYC at that time, I can assure you that there was a gay life outside Manhattan! The men interviewed here are the same 'A-list' queens who thought they were better than anyone else during the 70s, and here they are again, wasting attitude. The film should have at least tried to cover larger topics, such as race, ageism, the burgeoning gay 'caste system' based on wealth, body image, and the rise of the 'clones', discrimination of sub-groups, etc., but it only focuses on New York City. Moreover, even in that setting, it ignored the rest of the country. The film's attempt to focus on a specific place failed because it completely missed the broader context. Furthermore, while it captures some of life in the bars and discos we haunted, it's impossible to tell whether those places were representative of the entire city or just isolated examples. Additionally, the documentary's attempt to capture the spirit of the times through its interviews with the interviewees is laughable and regrettable. Finally, while the film does include some interesting footage from the bars and discos we haunted, it seems like the filmmakers wasted most of their time and effort on the interviews with the 'A-list queens' who were interviewed in a manner that seemed more suited to a talk show host interview than a serious documentary subject. Moreover, even these people are not as diverse as they could be; many of them are just average middle-aged cisgender white men or cisgender straight women wearing drag. The film's attempt at authenticity falls flat. Additionally, while the film does include some interesting facts about the history of drag in New York City and its impact on society during that time period, it seems like those facts were included more for the sake of having facts rather than exploring them in depth. Moreover, even these facts are presented in a manner that feels more suited to a summary list than a coherent narrative. The film's overall structure is disjointed and unfocused, making it difficult to stay engaged with its content. However, while the film does include some interesting footage from the bars and discos we haunted, it seems like the filmmakers wasted most of their time and effort on the interviews with the 'A-list queens' who were interviewed in a manner that seemed more suited to a talk show host interview than a serious documentary subject. Moreover, even these people are not as diverse as they could be; many of them are just average middle-aged cisgender white men or cisgender straight women wearing drag. The film's attempt at authenticity falls flat. Additionally, while the film does include some interesting facts about the history of drag in New York City and its impact on society during that time period, it seems like those facts were included more for the sake of having facts rather than exploring them in depth. Moreover, even these facts are presented in a manner that feels more suited to a summary list than a coherent narrative. The film's overall structure is disjointed and unfocused, making it difficult to stay engaged with its content. Finally, while the film does include some interesting footage from the bars and discos we haunted, it seems like the filmmakers wasted most of their time and effort on the interviews with the 'A-list queens' who were interviewed in a manner that seemed more suited to a talk show host interview than a serious documentary subject. Moreover, even these people are not as diverse as they could be; many of them are just average middle-aged cisgender white men or cisgender